## The HTTP SERVER effect, a server handle, and two performers.
##
## ONE operation, `serve`, which receives a HANDLER and runs it. The handler is
## a function from `Request` to `Response`; routing is not part of the effect.
## A route table is pure code that FOLDS into one handler (see
## `std::effects::http_router`), so the effect never grows an operation per
## routing feature.
##
## Two performers answer it. `MockHttpServer` binds no socket and does not call
## the handler — a test calls the handler itself, because a handler is a plain
## function. `HelidonHttpServer` binds a real socket and runs the handler on
## every request that arrives. Swapping one for the other changes the `perform`
## line and nothing else.
##
## This mirrors the client exactly, inverted. The client's `request` is a
## function you CALL; the server's `serve` takes a function you PROVIDE. Both
## speak the same `Request`/`Response` vocabulary.
##
## ASYNC. The handler's effect row is polymorphic (`<...>`), and that is
## load-bearing. A performer MAY install an async performer around the handler
## call, which discharges the handler's `<Async>`; `MockHttpServer` does not, so
## the row stays residual and the TEST supplies its own async:
##
##   perform LoomAsync in perform MockHttpServer in serve { handler = h }
##
## `HelidonHttpServer` runs the handler on a worker thread, carrying the handler
## chain that was in force at `serve`. So the performers a handler finds are the
## ones the surrounding program installed — which is what makes the row more
## than a formality.
##
## Effects other than async always escape through `serve` to whoever installed
## the server.

use std::effects::http::{ Method, Get, Post, Put, Delete, Patch, Head, Options, Request, Response }
use std::collections::map
use std::collections::iter
use std::collections::bytes
use std::str

## A running server. `stop` shuts it down; `port` is the bound port, which
## matters when a test binds port 0 and the OS chooses.
pub type ServerHandle = {
  stop: {} -> {},
  port: Int,
}

## Run a handler as a server.
effect HttpServer where {
  serve: { handler: { req: Request } -> Response <...> } -> ServerHandle <HttpServer>
}

## A server that never binds a socket.
##
## `serve` returns a handle whose `stop` does nothing and whose `port` is 0. The
## handler is NOT called here — a test calls it directly, which is the whole
## point: a handler is a plain function and needs no server to exercise.
##
## It installs NO async performer, so a handler's `<Async>` escapes to the test.
impl HttpServer for MockHttpServer where {
  serve = { handler } -> { stop = {} -> {}, port = 0 }
}

## Build a `Request` for a handler, without a server.
##
## This is the test seam. A router is pure and a handler is pure, so the whole
## request path is exercised by passing one of these in.
pub let incoming: { method: Method, url: Str } -> Request = { method, url } -> {
  method = method,
  url = url,
  headers = map::empty {},
  body = None,
  timeout_ms = None,
  chunks = None,
}

## `incoming` carrying a STREAMED body, for exercising a streaming handler
## without a socket.
##
## The test seam has to cover both shapes or a streaming handler is only ever
## tested against a real server — which is the position `MockHttpServer` exists
## to avoid. `chunks` is any `Iter Bytes`, so a test says what the chunk
## boundaries are, and boundaries are the whole difficulty of streaming: a
## reader that happens to work on one chunk is not a reader.
pub let incoming_streamed
  : { method: Method, url: Str, chunks: Iter Bytes }
  -> Request
  = { method, url, chunks } -> {
    method = method,
    url = url,
    headers = map::empty {},
    body = None,
    timeout_ms = None,
    chunks = Some { val = chunks },
  }

## `incoming` for a GET, which is most of what tests want.
pub let get_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Get, url = url }

## `incoming` for each remaining method.
# Each returns a plain Request, so Request's own chain carries on from here
# (`.with_text {}`, `.with_header {}`, `.with_json {}`) and `.to {}` applies the
# result to a handler — one left-to-right line instead of a call nested inside a
# call inside a record.
pub let post_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Post, url = url }
pub let put_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Put, url = url }
pub let delete_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Delete, url = url }
pub let patch_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Patch, url = url }
pub let head_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Head, url = url }
pub let options_request: { url: Str } -> Request = { url } ->
  incoming { method = Options, url = url }

## ── A server that binds a socket ────────────────────────────────────────────

## What a real server needs to know before it binds.
##
## `port = 0` lets the OS choose, and the chosen port is readable from the
## handle afterwards — which is how a test talks to a server without reserving
## a port number nobody else may use.
##
## `workers = 0` means "pick a sensible number". A worker is a virtual thread
## that runs handlers, so the default is wide rather than core-count-sized: a
## handler that performs a request of its own occupies its worker while it
## waits.
## `stream_bodies` hands each handler its request body as `chunks` instead of
## reading it into `body` first, so an upload's peak cost is one chunk rather
## than its whole size.
##
## It is a server-wide switch and OFF by default, which is a decision worth
## stating. A per-request rule — stream when Content-Length is over some
## threshold — sounds better and is worse: every handler would then have to read
## BOTH shapes, and a handler that reads only `body` would silently find `None`
## exactly for the large uploads it was meant to handle. One switch means a
## handler knows which shape it gets, and `body_bytes` covers the handler that
## wants the whole thing anyway.
pub type ServerConfig = {
  port: Int,
  host: Str,
  workers: Int,
  stream_bodies: Bool,
}

## Port 8080 on the loopback address, with the default worker count.
##
## Loopback and not `0.0.0.0`: a server reachable from the network is a
## decision, and a default should not make it for you.
pub let default_server_config: ServerConfig = {
  port = 8080,
  host = "127.0.0.1",
  workers = 0,
  stream_bodies = False,
}

## ── Streamed request bodies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

## A single-shot reader over one request's body.
##
## Opaque, like `FileHandle`: the value is whatever the server put behind it,
## and the only thing to do with one is read the next chunk. It is SINGLE-SHOT —
## the body is consumed as it is read, so two walks of the same `chunks` see the
## first one's leftovers.
##
## It outlives the handler. Helidon keeps the connection's content readable
## while the response is written, so a handler may hand its request's `chunks`
## straight on as the response's and get a pass-through that buffers nothing —
## measured at 20 B, 40 KB and 400 KB. `HttpStreamedUploadTest` pins it.
pub newtype BodyReader = __NativeBodyReader

## How much is asked for per read. One chunk is the peak cost of streaming, so
## this is the number that makes an upload cheap; 64 KiB is a socket read that
## is worth making.
let chunk_bytes: Int = 65536

## The reader as an `Iter Bytes`, pulled a chunk at a time.
##
## `iter::unfold` over a native read, which is exactly `response_file`'s shape
## one direction over — and the reason this is written in Mezze rather than
## built by the native: `unfold`'s step has to be a Mezze function.
##
## A read error THROWS rather than ending the iterator. `response_file` maps its
## `Err` to `Done`, which for a download means a short file; for an UPLOAD the
## same choice would make a client that vanished mid-body look like one that
## finished, and a handler would commit a truncated upload believing it whole.
let chunks_of_reader: { reader: BodyReader } -> Iter Bytes = { reader } ->
  iter::unfold {
    state = {},
    step = { s } -> do
      let b = __native_http_request_read_chunk { reader = reader, max_bytes = chunk_bytes }
      if bytes::size { b = b } == 0 then Done else Yield { val = b, next = {} }
  }

## Give the request its `chunks` when the server handed over a reader.
##
## The native always passes a `reader` field, `None` unless the server was asked
## to stream, so one wrapper serves both and the handler sees an ordinary
## `Request` either way. `serve`'s own type is unchanged — this wrapper lives
## inside `start_socket` and is not part of what a caller provides.
let attach_reader: { req: Request, reader: Option BodyReader } -> Request = { req, reader } ->
  match reader is
    None -> req
    Some { val = r } -> { ...req, chunks = Some { val = chunks_of_reader { reader = r } } }

## Bind the socket, start the workers, hand back the handle.
let start_socket
  : { handler: { req: Request } -> Response <...>, config: ServerConfig }
  -> ServerHandle
  = { handler, config } -> do
    let wrapped = { req, reader } -> handler { req = attach_reader { req = req, reader = reader } }
    let s = __native_http_server_start {
      handler = wrapped,
      port = config.port,
      host = config.host,
      workers = config.workers,
      stream_bodies = config.stream_bodies,
    }
    {
      stop = {} -> __native_http_server_stop { server = s },
      port = __native_http_server_port { server = s },
    }

## A server on a real socket, backed by Helidon.
##
## The handler runs on a worker thread that Truffle owns, carrying the handler
## chain that was in force at `serve`. So a handler may perform the effects the
## surrounding program installed — `Time`, `Log`, `HttpClient` — and finds the
## same performers it would find anywhere else in that scope.
impl HttpServer for HelidonHttpServer where {
  serve = { handler } -> start_socket { handler = handler, config = default_server_config },
}

## `HelidonHttpServer` with a config. `port = 0` is what a test wants.
impl HttpServer for HelidonHttpServerWith { config: ServerConfig } where {
  serve = { handler } -> start_socket { handler = handler, config = config },
}
